  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe key feature we'll be monitoring this week is the tropical \ndisturbance located south of the Mississippi River Delta as of \nSunday afternoon, which presently has a medium (50%) chance of \norganizing into a tropical depression or storm. This disturbance \nis expected to be guided by the flow along the southern periphery \nof the ridge towards the Upper Texas coast by about \nTuesday/Wednesday. Most models take the system towards Southeast \nand East Texas before curving north, but as the subtropical ridge\nslides farther east, steering winds are forecast to slacken. This\ncould result in the disturbance drifting west closer to our area \nonce inland if it doesn't catch the northward flow on the backside\nof the ridge. There is still substantial variance in outcomes, so\nfor now the main message is that we'll likely see an increase in \ncloud cover and rain chances primarily along and east of I-35 late\nTuesday through Thursday. Rain chances could linger into Friday, \nthough this would likely require a more sluggish track along the \nwestern side of the current model guidance distribution similar to\nthat depicted by the GFS/AIGFS.\n\nThe disturbance is small in size, so for now rain chances in the \nmidweek period are still below 40 percent over the Coastal Plains. \nTemperatures could moderate more if the disturbance comes closer,\nbut its stature and uncertainty result in relatively modest \nimprint in the average national model blend with highs still \nprojected to remain in the upper 90s with occasional spots in the \nlow 100s. More aggressive cloud cover or showers would bring highs\ncloser to the low 90s as depicted in the 10th percentile NBM.\n\nThe weekend forecast hinges on the ultimate fate of the tropical \ndisturbance, but the broader pattern suggests a general weakening of \nridging across the southern tier of the US. This leaves the \nenvironment over South Central Texas more open to seabreeze showers \nand isolated inland convective development, either associated with\nor independent of the tropical disturbance. Thus, the forecast \nmaintains low to medium rain chances next weekend, with the best \nchances over the Coastal Plains and lower chances tapering off \ntowards the I-35 corridor and Hill Country.",
